WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.480 --> 00:00:11.520
When Huey had me elected to a state senators’ job as a student at LSU, I was not eligible.

2
00:00:11.520 --> 00:00:19.780
But I remember [Coach Lawrence] “Biff” Jones and Troy Middleton, “Don’t get involved because he just did it as a prank.”

3
00:00:19.780 --> 00:00:35.200
What he wanted to do was have me elected as state senator from Baton Rouge and he wanted me to come to the legislature and pass legislation benefiting the LSU football team.

4
00:00:35.200 --> 00:00:45.520
So we had . . . You had those kind of people to fall back on and guide you, but Huey was a great guy.

5
00:00:45.520 --> 00:00:48.280
I had a beautiful relationship with him.

6
00:00:48.280 --> 00:00:53.980
Because here I was a country boy from Mississippi and here was this . . . the Governor of Louisiana,

7
00:00:53.980 --> 00:00:56.980
State Senator, who took a liking to me.

8
00:00:56.980 --> 00:00:59.700
He . . . When I got hurt in the Tulane game,

9
00:00:59.700 --> 00:01:08.800
he came the next day to the Infirmary and visited with me for over an hour, telling me what his dreams and plans were for LSU.

10
00:01:08.800 --> 00:01:15.140
He just . . . He aspired to be an athlete, but he never was.

11
00:01:15.140 --> 00:01:22.600
But he would have given . . . I think he would’ve almost given up his governorship if he could have been a good football player.

12
00:01:22.600 --> 00:01:27.180
Do you remember what he said that his hopes and dreams for LSU were when he was talking to you?

13
00:01:27.180 --> 00:01:30.440
Well, he wanted LSU to be a major university.

14
00:01:30.440 --> 00:01:34.460
He wanted LSU to have a first class medical school.

15
00:01:34.460 --> 00:01:37.907
And he wanted LSU to beat Alabama [laughs].

16
00:01:37.907 --> 00:01:41.320
[laughs] Did you beat Alabama that year?

17
00:01:41.320 --> 00:01:44.100
Well, we did not.

18
00:01:44.100 --> 00:01:45.740
Alabama was never on our schedule.

19
00:01:45.740 --> 00:01:46.920
Okay.

20
00:01:46.920 --> 00:01:48.340
We knew him as an individual.

21
00:01:48.340 --> 00:01:59.080
He’d come out to the practice and talk with us and at times he’d . . . I remember one time he came over to see me and he wanted to know how I held the ball,

22
00:01:59.080 --> 00:01:59.420
how I threw it and so forth.

23
00:01:59.420 --> 00:02:03.260
I gave him all the details.

24
00:02:03.260 --> 00:02:07.700
He walked over to Burt Gates and he said, “Burt, that’s not the way you do.”

25
00:02:07.700 --> 00:02:11.940
He repeated everything I had just told him, and then walked off as if, you know,

26
00:02:11.940 --> 00:02:17.240
he’d done a great . . . [laughing] He just loved to be part of the . . . part of the team.

27
00:02:17.240 --> 00:02:25.880
First thing he did when he hit the campus, he’d call out Castro Carazo and the band to meet him at the Greek Theater.

28
00:02:25.880 --> 00:02:28.700
And he’d ask the football players to come there.

29
00:02:28.700 --> 00:02:35.480
He’d lead the band and . . . He loved the band and he loved the football team.

30
00:02:35.480 --> 00:02:42.220
I remember one time, he brought me . . . At that time, Liberty Magazine,

31
00:02:42.220 --> 00:02:48.120
which is now out of circulation, would always print the winning play of the week.

32
00:02:48.120 --> 00:03:01.440
Well, he would take that diagram and redesign it and bring it to LSU and ask me if he thought that we could work that play. [laughs] He just . . . Really,

33
00:03:01.440 --> 00:03:05.760
you know, that’s the part of him I got to know and appreciate.

34
00:03:05.760 --> 00:03:15.000
I knew what he was doing for the boys and girls of Louisiana, so I was not privileged to know about his political life.
